  ],
  "text": "A surgical robot removes the natural tremor in a surgeon's hand entirely. Every human hand shakes a little. It is normal, it is involuntary, and it never stops. At the scale of stitching a blood vessel, that tremor is enormous. So the machine reads the movement, strips the shake out, and scales the rest down. Move your hand a centimetre and the instrument moves a millimetre. Perfectly smoothly. The surgeon is still driving every part of it. Nothing here is autonomous. Industrial arms do the other version. The same movement, to fractions of a millimetre, for years. No fatigue, no drift, no bad morning. That is a kind of precision no person has. And some of them work in places where a person simply could not survive. These are not replacements. They are amplifiers, with a human still holding the controls. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
